From 1a5083899c7dddef8d5006c188530b540364119c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dong Chenchen <superdcc97@163.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 23:42:40 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 2/4] bpf: add optional ordered domains to CPUMAP

Changing an active flow's CPUMAP target can deliver its new packets before
the old target has finished processing earlier ones. A ring dequeue or a
remote XDP PASS is too early to authorize that change: GRO and the normal
receive list can still hold packets.

Let a nonzero map_extra allocate a fixed number of ordering domains.
bpf_cpumap_redirect() accepts a BPF-selected domain and candidate CPU;
actual redirect atomically reserves a submission on the retained target.
Outstanding source bulks or an incomplete old watermark defer migration.
The kfunc only records intent, so returning a different XDP action leaves
no reservation. Ordinary redirect_map calls reject ordered maps.

Assign tickets at successful ring insertion under its producer lock and
return source reservations on both insertion and failure. Preserve mixed
native/generic ring order, and publish a completed prefix only after a
full GRO flush and synchronous stack handoff, every 64 packets or at ring
empty. Remote DROP retires tickets; a second remote REDIRECT is dropped
because its asynchronous completion is outside this accounting domain.

Retain references to retired entries' progress while their resources drain
through the existing RCU/kthread teardown. A replaced entry cannot accept
new submissions, and reusing a CPU index cannot bypass the old generation.
An offline previous CPU permits leaving its watermark behind once source
reservations settle, matching the scope of native RFS's offline exception.
Existing CPUMAP worker draining is retained, without a discard-all path.

bpf_cpumap_get_state() exposes an informational snapshot for observation
and drain after ingress quiescence. Flow identity, socket hints and CPU
policy remain in BPF. Ordinary maps allocate no domains or progress state.
No CONFIG_RPS dependency or driver change is introduced.

Validated with a full CONFIG_RPS=n build and ordered CPUMAP selftests under
KASAN/lockdep. Compile checks cover CONFIG_RPS=y, CONFIG_INET=n,
CONFIG_BPF_SYSCALL=n and PREEMPT_RT. Throughput and GRO aggregation still
require measurements on physical NICs.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Dong Chenchen <superdcc97@163.com>
